00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.570
- This episode of Wheel of Science

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:04.750
is brought to you by Wix.

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:06.070
- On this episode of Wheel of Science

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:07.990
we're talking politics in space

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:11.820
or Space Force, but you
gotta say it differently.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.740
- Yeah, in space there
are only deep voices.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:15.573
Space Force.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:20.253
- This is Wheel of Science.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:21.953
- Hey everybody, what's up,

00:00:21.953 --> 00:00:23.100
and welcome to Wheel of Science.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.590
I'm your host Chuck Nice,

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:27.790
and this is the show where
we answer your questions

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:28.970
about the universe.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:30.810
Actually, I don't answer
your questions about

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.650
the universe, but that would be silly.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.190
The one, the only Doctor
Neil deGrasse Tyson

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:38.650
answers your questions about the universe.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:39.600
- Hey Chuck.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.530
- What's up Neil?

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.370
- Doing good. You found
me in my office today.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:43.720
- I think this is the longest time that

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.440
you have been home.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.391
- Yeah. Sometimes, I gotta
give the universe a rest.

00:00:48.391 --> 00:00:50.230
- I disagree with that.

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:52.608
I think the universe can handle you, Neil.

00:00:52.608 --> 00:00:53.441
(laughs)

00:00:53.441 --> 00:00:54.650
So Neil, before we get to the questions,

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:55.630
why don't you just tell us,

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:57.560
what is Space Force.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.010
- Yeah if that comes to past,

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:02.050
it would just be a
reorganization of the military

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.480
with a lot of the activities that

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:04.571
have already been happening

00:01:04.571 --> 00:01:07.429
under the auspices of the Air Force.

00:01:07.429 --> 00:01:09.810
Many people, I don't think knew,

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:13.670
the Air Force has a U.S.
Space Command within it.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.170
And they're the ones that
conceived and launched

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.280
the GPS satellites.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:22.300
They, with NSA and others,
have spy satellites in orbit.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.650
This been going on since the 60's.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:25.890
Spy satellites.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.710
So, this concept that
the military use of space

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.880
is something new, no it's something old.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.940
And all this would do would
codify it into a new branch

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:36.240
of the Armed Forces.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.950
- So it really already exists.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.960
It's like calling an
apple pie an apple crisp.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.820
- Taking apple pie and hitting
it with a hammer a few times

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:44.151
and call it apple crisp?

00:01:44.151 --> 00:01:46.120
(laughing)

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.627
- That's exactly what I meant.

00:01:47.627 --> 00:01:49.851
(laughing)

00:01:49.851 --> 00:01:51.500
Neil, what time is it my friend?

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.880
- It is time to spend the wheel.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.366
- My, how formal.

00:01:56.366 --> 00:01:59.949
(playful electronic music)

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.200
- Mike Maas wants to know
"If, and, when we colonize

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:09.110
another planet, who has
political rights over that body?"

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:12.940
- Welcome to the wild
frontier of space law.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.620
It's not clear.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:16.760
The Outer Space Treaty stipulates that

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.150
you can own anything in space,

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.811
so nations cannot declare it there own.

00:02:22.811 --> 00:02:26.910
So, if that holds up the answer is

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:29.070
nobody has jurisdiction,

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:34.070
but I think laws imagined many decades ago

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.230
may need to be updated and modified given

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.060
what we learn and know about the future of

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.300
space exploration.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.090
So, we're in need of some
enlightened attorneys

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:45.860
to think this through going forward.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.650
- Oh God, more lawyers.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.230
- No, better lawyers.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:50.860
- So you mentioned space treaty.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:51.693
What is that?

00:02:51.693 --> 00:02:53.690
- The Outer Space Treaty
was a U.N. document

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:55.800
drafted in the late 1960s,

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.990
ratified by, today,
more than 100 countries,

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:03.010
and it's a guideline for the
peaceful use of outer space,

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:04.810
and it's very kumbaya,

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:06.390
you know if your astronaut is in trouble

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:08.886
I come help you and I bring
you food and water and

00:03:08.886 --> 00:03:13.550
it's very beautiful, it's
very future thinking,

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.790
but it leaves open the
possibility that you

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:19.510
might want to defend your assets in space,

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:22.080
with some form of weapon or another.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.520
Suppose you see a
satellite coming toward you

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.470
and you think its gonna hurt you.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.580
Can you preemptively take it out so that

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:29.130
you don't get hurt.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.442
This is the fuzzy area in that document,

00:03:31.442 --> 00:03:34.520
but that doesn't subtract
from the fact that

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:38.660
it really is imagining a
future, a very peaceful future,

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:40.200
of cooperation in space,

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.510
which I think in the end is a good idea.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:44.170
- Look at that, Gene
Roddenberry couldn't have done

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:45.450
a better job himself.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:46.300
- Yep!

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:47.133
- All right, Neil.

00:03:47.133 --> 00:03:47.966
You ready for another one?

00:03:47.966 --> 00:03:48.848
